\section{How specific a key is}{

\code{n_eins_sharing} counts the EINs holding the key, across the whole database.
A key held by one EIN can propose a candidate on its own; a key held by
thousands (\verb{1 WATERFOWL WAY|38120}, \code{ptk.org}) can only confirm one found
another way.

The whole-database count answers "is this key ambiguous in the universe".
A matcher comparing against a selection of layers needs the count within the
selection, so \code{\link[=md_build_ein_key]{md_build_ein_key()}} also writes \code{ein_key_layer}: the count per
\verb{(kind, key, layer)}. Layers partition EINs - each EIN has exactly one - so
the count for any set of layers is the exact sum of its rows.
}

